 the theory with the highest empirical content and thus the greatest capacity for future falsification; this preference survives only if one accepts the cost of abandoning the goal of 'justified belief' in favor of 'optimal testability'"}, {"block": "3cc505d4422a10e3129c235999eea3a50d57daa1dfbc86d697bf285702aca4a7", "quote": "It posits that choosing the best-corroborated theory is equivalent to choosing the most severely tested survivor, which logically implies it has withstood the strongest attempts at refutation. This avoids induction because it makes no claim about the future performance of the theory based on past success, but rather selects the theory that currently offers the most precise targets for potential error."}], "successor_question": "If two theories have equal empirical content but different corroboration histories, what non-inductive reason does Popper's methodology give for preferring the better-corroborated one?"}